<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivo Andrić</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1892–1975</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivo Andrić was a Yugoslav novelist and diplomat born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>. His novels, above all The Bridge on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drina">Drina</a> (1945), trace the history of Bosnia under Ottoman and Austrian rule. He served as a diplomat of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, including as envoy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in 1939–1941, and received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1961.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivo-andric.jpg" alt="Ivo Andrić" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stevan Kragujević · CC BY-SA 3.0 rs · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S._Kragujevic,_Ivo_Andric,_1961_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
